ngenn8le09ob03re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "engenn8le09ob03re2img01.jpg", "text": "", "id": 0}, {"time": "00100", "image": "engenn8le09ob03re2img01.jpg", "text": "The first step to Crisis Management sounds easy: do not have one! Many avoidable crises happen because preparation has not been done. We want to learn through mistakes to avoid similar problems in the future.\nNow, let's say that a problem comes to our attention. This is the second step, the stage of detection.", "id": 1}, {"time": "22000", "image": "engenn8le09ob03re2img02.jpg", "text": "Detecting the problem early is very important. We respond immediately\nand move to step three: containment of the problem. A quick and appropriate response for damage control is necessary to prevent the problem from getting worse.\nFourth, a solution must be made to deal with the problem. This is about recovery. This step is usually the longest but one must remember that it is near the end.", "id": 2}, {"time": "50000", "image": "engenn8le09ob03re2img03.jpg", "text": "If the answer were quick or easy, it would not have been a crisis in the first place.\nFinally, once the problem has been resolved, we need to learn from the incident. A crisis brings learning opportunities. It enables us to manage small challenges before they become bigger ones. And so we come back around to the first step in preparing to avert the next crisis.", "id": 3}]}}], "template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "audio", "id": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}]}]}]}